<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Small rounded chip used by the header stats (level / steps / scrap), the
  three tiles beside the hatch panel, and every home-screen nav button.

  The fill is a very shallow top-to-bottom gradient rather than flat white:
  lighter at the top, a touch of warmth at the bottom, which is what reads as a
  surface catching light from above. Both ends stay inside the cream palette -
  the difference is a few percent, and it is meant to be felt rather than seen.

  The drop shadow is not here. A shape has no shadow of its own; it comes from
  elevation on the view wearing this, whose outline Android takes from the
  bottom layer's own corners - see the note on that below.

  The rim is a small brass pipe's own cross-section now, not a single flat
  stroke: five concentric rounded-rect outlines at shrinking width, the same
  five-tone recipe (rim shadow, core shadow, lit copper body, brass sheen,
  a near-white specular line) hatch_tunnel.xml already uses for the actual
  pipe the rat travels down, just traced around a tile's own rounded corners
  instead of along that pipe's centreline. "A little brass pipe running
  round the tiles" was asked for directly; this is the same physical bevel
  that drawable already renders, reused rather than a second technique
  invented to say the same thing.

  Every ring layer after the base is stroke-only and colour-referenced, not
  a literal hex, so it needs no drawable-night counterpart of its own -
  shadow_warm is already themed, and copper/copper_shadow/brass_bright/cream
  are deliberately not (see values-night/colors.xml's own comment: a metal
  tone is meant to look the same regardless of theme). Only the base layer's
  fill still needs one, for the same #F8F0E1-is-a-literal reason
  drawable-night/bg_pill_tan.xml's own comment already gives.

  Elevation note: a LayerDrawable takes its auto-generated outline (what
  gives a view's elevation an actual shadow to cast) from its bottom-most
  layer, which is why that one is still a plain filled shape at the full
  20dp radius and carries no stroke of its own - every ring drawn on top of
  it is purely decorative and never touches what the view's own lift looks
  like.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <gradient
                android:angle="270"
                android:startColor="@color/card_white"
                android:endColor="#F8F0E1"
                android:type="linear" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <stroke android:width="3.4dp" android:color="@color/shadow_warm" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <stroke android:width="2.6dp" android:color="@color/copper_shadow" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <stroke android:width="1.8dp" android:color="@color/copper" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <stroke android:width="0.9dp" android:color="@color/brass_bright" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <stroke android:width="0.5dp" android:color="@color/cream" />
            <corners android:radius="20dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

</layer-list>
